--TEST--
`Core\Command::help` renders a usage page from the table `#[Command]` built while compiling, for one command and for the program — `rule:tooling/commands-are-compiled`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 6 builds the table from the declaration itself, so every line below is
// derived from the same parameter list the call is checked against — the usage
// text and the parser cannot drift, because there is only one of them.
class Deployer {
    #[Core\Command(name: "deploy", about: "Push the current build")]
    public static function deploy(
        string $target,
        #[Core\Option(short: "n", about: "Print what would happen")] bool $dryRun = false,
    ): uint {
        return 0;
    }

    #[Core\Command(name: "version")]
    public static function version(): uint {
        return 0;
    }
}

// A `Cli\Text` renders as the bytes it already holds (`rule:security/capture-answers-the-carrier`). An option
// that wrote a `short:` claims two spellings: the usage line summarizes it by
// the long one and the `options:` block names both.
//
// The middle column is each parameter's declared type, which the table does
// not carry: the page reaches the handler's own signature through the
// `Class::method` label the row holds. `$dryRun` names `bool` and not
// `bool = false`, because § 6 gives an option declared `bool` by its being
// written and the declaration's own default is not what an unwritten flag
// takes.
echo "" . Core\Command::help("deploy");
echo "--\n";
// `null` is the program's own page: which commands there are, rather than what
// one of them takes. A command that wrote no `about:` gets no invented one.
echo "" . Core\Command::help(null);
--EXPECT--
usage: deploy <target> [--dryRun]

Push the current build

arguments:
  <target>  string

options:
  -n, --dryRun  bool  Print what would happen
--
usage: <command> [arguments]

commands:
  deploy   Push the current build
  version
